WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.370
Some of you may have seen in school that the
surface area of a sphere is 4pi*R^2, a suspiciously

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.429
suggestive formula given that it’s an clean
multiple of pi*R^2, the area of a circle with

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:20.630
the same radius. But have you ever wondered
why is this true? And I don’t just mean

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:26.739
proving this 4pi*R^2 formula, I mean viscerally
feeling a connection between this surface

00:00:26.739 --> 00:00:29.480
area, and these four circles.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.950
How lovely would it be if there was some shift
in perspective that showed how you could nicely

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:39.699
and perfectly fit these four circles onto
the sphere’s surface? Nothing can be quite

00:00:39.699 --> 00:00:43.579
that simple, since the curvature of a sphere’s
surface is different from the curvature of

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:51.269
a flat plane, which is why trying to fit paper
around a sphere doesn’t really work. Nevertheless,

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:56.269
I’d like to show you two ways of thinking
about this surface area connecting it in a

00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:02.839
satisfying way to these circles. The first
is a classic, one of the true gems of geometry

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:10.620
all students should experience. The second
line of reasoning is something of my own which

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.650
draws a more direct line between the sphere
and its shadow.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.030
And lastly I’ll share why this four-fold
relation is not unique to spheres, but is

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:26.180
instead one specific instance of a much more
general fact for all convex shapes in 3d.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.761
Starting with a birds eye view here, the idea
for the first approach is to show that the

00:01:29.761 --> 00:01:35.000
surface area of the sphere is the same as
the area of a cylinder with the same radius

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.711
and the same height as the sphere. Or rather,
a cylinder without its top and bottom, what

00:01:40.711 --> 00:01:46.820
you might call the “label” of that cylinder.
With that, we can unwrap that label to understand

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.140
it as a simple rectangle.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:55.490
The width of this rectangle comes from the
cylinder’s circumference, so it’s 2*pi*R,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:01.480
and the height comes from the height of the
sphere, which is 2R. This already gives the

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:08.979
formula, 4pi*R^2, but in the spirit of mathematical
playfulness it’s nice to see how four circles

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:16.459
with radius R fit into this. The idea is that
you can unwrap each circle into a triangle,

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:22.200
without changing its area, and fit these nicely
onto our unfolded cylinder label. More on

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.719
that in a bit.

00:02:23.719 --> 00:02:28.599
The more pressing question is why on earth
the sphere can be related to the cylinder.

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:35.540
This animation is already suggestive of how
this works. The idea is to approximate the

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:41.489
area of the sphere with many tiny rectangles
covering it, and to show how if you project

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:46.540
those little rectangles directly outward,
as if casting a shadow by little lights positioned

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:52.499
on the z-axis pointing parallel to the xy
plane, the projection of each rectangle on

00:02:52.499 --> 00:02:58.780
the cylinder, quite surprisingly, ends up
having the same area as the original rectangle.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:05.019
But why should that be? Well, there are two
competing effects at play here. For one of

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:09.739
these rectangles, let’s call the side along
the latitude lines its width, and the side

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:15.730
along the longitude lines its height. On the
one hand, as this rectangle is projected outward,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:23.690
its width will get scaled up. For rectangles
towards the poles, that length is scaled quite

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:31.180
a bit, since they’re projected over a longer
distance. For those closer to the equator,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.579
less so

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:39.480
But on the other hand, because these rectangles
are at a slant with respect to the z-direction,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.220
during this projection the height of each
such rectangle will get scaled down. Think

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.560
about holding some flat object and looking
at its shadow. As you reorient that object,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.969
the shadow looks more or less squished for
some angles. Those rectangles towards the

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:02.019
poles are quite slanted in this way, so their
height gets squished a lot. For those closer

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:07.200
to the equator, less so.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.209
It will turn out that these two effects, of
stretching the width and squishing the height,

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:16.639
cancel each other out perfectly.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:20.509
Already as a rough sketch, wouldn’t you
agree this is a very pretty way of reasoning?

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:25.199
Of course, the meat here comes from showing
why these two competing effects on each rectangle

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:29.960
cancel out perfectly. In some ways, the details
fleshing this out are just as pretty as the

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:34.660
zoomed out structure of the full argument.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.670
Let me go ahead and cut away half the sphere
so we get get a better look. For any mathematical

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:44.510
problem solving it never hurts to start by
giving names to things. Let’s say the radius

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:50.000
of the sphere is R. Focus on one specific
rectangle, and let’s call the distance between

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.670
our rectangle and the z-axis is d. You could
complain that this distance d is a little

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:01.740
ambiguous depending on which point of the
rectangle you’re going from, but for tinier

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:07.430
and tinier rectangles that ambiguity will
be negligible. And tinier and tinier is when

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.810
this approximation-with-rectangles gets closer
to the true surface area anyway. To choose

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.639
an arbitrary standard let’s say d is the
distance from the bottom of the rectangle.

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:24.440
To think about projecting out to the cylinder,
picture two similar triangles. This first

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:30.550
one shares its base with the base of the rectangle
on the sphere, and has a tip at the same height

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:37.410
on the z-axis a distance d-away. The second
is a scaled up version of this, scaled so

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:43.409
that it just barely reaches the cylinder,
meaning its long side now has length R. So

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:48.520
the ratio of their bases, which is how much
our rectangle’s width gets stretched out,

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.710
is R/d.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:57.919
What about the height? How precisely does
that get scaled down as we project? Again,

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:02.849
let’s slice a cross section here. In fact,
why don’t we go ahead and completely focus

00:06:02.849 --> 00:06:05.680
our view to this 2d cross section.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.310
To think about the projection, let’s make
a little right triangle like this, where what

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:15.720
was the height of our spherical rectangle
is the hypotenuse, and its projection is one

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.080
of the legs. Pro tip, anytime you’re doing
geometry with circles or spheres, keep at

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.530
the forefront of your mind that anything tangent
to the circle is perpendicular to the radius

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:34.960
drawn to that point of tangency. It’s crazy
how helpful that one little fact can be. Once

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:41.259
we draw that radial line, together with the
distance d we have another right triangle.

00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:45.550
Often in geometry, I like to imagine tweaking
the parameters of a setup and imagining how

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:51.580
the relevant shapes change; this helps to
make guesses about what relations there are.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.469
In this case, you might predict that the two
triangles I’ve drawn are similar to each

00:06:55.469 --> 00:07:01.759
other, since their shapes change in concert
with each other. This is indeed true, but

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:06.360
as always, don’t take my word for it, see
if you can justify this for yourself.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:12.770
Again, it never hurts to give more names to
things. Maybe call this angle alpha and this

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:20.539
one beta. Since this is a right triangle,
you know that alpha + beta + 90 degrees = 180

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:28.030
degrees. Now zoom in to our little triangle,
and see if we can figure out its angles. You

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:40.669
have 90 degrees + beta + (some angle) forming
a straight line. So that little angle must

00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:47.780
be alpha. This lets us fill in a few more
values, revealing that this little triangle

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.949
has the same angles, alpha and beta, as the
big one. So they are indeed similar.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:59.139
Deep in the weeds it’s sometimes easy to
forget why we’re doing this. We want to

00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:04.909
know how much the height of our sphere-rectangle
gets squished during this projection, which

00:08:04.909 --> 00:08:11.289
is the ratio of this hypotenuse to the leg
on the right. By the similarity with the big

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:14.530
triangle, that ratio is R/d.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:20.070
So indeed, as this rectangle gets projected
outward onto the cylinder, the effect of stretching

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:25.080
out the width is perfectly canceled out by
how much the height gets squished due to the

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.800
slant.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.030
As a fun sidenote, you might notice that it
looks like the projected rectangle is a 90

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:37.849
degree rotation of the original. This would
not be true in general, but by a lovely coincidence,

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:42.110
the way I’m parametrizing the sphere results
in rectangles where the ratio of the width

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:48.920
the the height starts out as d to R. So for
this very specific case, rescaling the width

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:57.000
by R/d and the height by d/R actually does
have the same effect as a 90 degree rotation.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.860
This lends itself to a rather bizarre way
to animate the relation, where instead of

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:12.930
projecting each rectangular piece, you rotate
each one and rearrange them to make the cylinder.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:20.480
Now, if you’re really thinking critically,
you might still not be satisfied that this

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:24.163
shows that the surface area of the sphere
equals the area of this cylinder label since

00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:29.490
these little rectangles only approximate the
relevant areas. Well, the idea is that this

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:35.490
approximation gets closer and closer to the
true value for finer and finer coverings.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.860
Since for any specific covering, the sphere
rectangles have the same area as the cylinder

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.490
rectangles, whatever values each of these
two series of approximations are approaching

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:47.290
must actually be the same.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:53.110
I mean, as you get really aggressively philosophical
about what we even mean by surface area, these

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:57.440
sorts of rectangular approximations and not
just aids in our problem-solving toolbox,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:05.160
they end up serving as a way of rigorously
defining the area of smooth curved surfaces.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.440
This kind of reasoning is essentially calculus,
just stated without any of the jargon. In

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:14.250
fact, I think neat geometric arguments like
this, which require no background in calculus

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:19.110
to understand, can serve as a great way to
tee things up for new calculus students so

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:24.930
that they have the core ideas before seeing
the definitions which make them precise, rather

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:28.870
than the other way around.
Unfold circle

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:34.571
So as I said before, if you’re itching to
see a direct connection to four circles, one

00:10:34.571 --> 00:10:40.320
nice way is to unwrap these circles into triangles.
If this is something you haven’t seen before,

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.680
I go into much more detail about why this
works in the first video of the calculus series.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:51.139
The basic idea is to relate thin concentric
rings of the circle with horizontal slices

00:10:51.139 --> 00:10:57.150
of this triangle. Because that circumference
of each such ring increases linearly in proportion

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:03.720
to the radius, always 2pi times that radius,
when you unwrap them all and line them up,

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:11.471
their ends will form a straight line, giving
you a triangle with a base of 2pi*R, and a

00:11:11.471 --> 00:11:13.490
height of R, as opposed to some other curved
shape.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:19.110
And four of these unwrapped circles fit into
our rectangle, which is in some sense an unwrapped

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:23.500
version of the sphere’s surface.
Second proof

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:28.310
Nevertheless, you might wonder if there’s
a way than this to relate the sphere directly

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:32.769
to a circle with the same radius, rather than
going through this intermediary of the cylinder.

00:11:32.769 --> 00:11:37.370
I do have a proof for you to this effect,
leveraging a little trigonometry, though I

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:40.829
have to admit I still think the comparison
to the cylinder wins out on elegance.

00:11:40.829 --> 00:11:47.280
I’m a big believer that the best way to
really learn math is to do problems yourself,

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.829
which is a bit hypocritical coming from a
channel essentially consisting of lectures.

00:11:50.829 --> 00:11:55.690
So I’m going to try something a little different
here and present the proof as a heavily guided

00:11:55.690 --> 00:12:00.380
sequence of exercises. Yes, I know that’s
less fun and it means you have to pull out

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:06.660
some paper to do some work, but I guarantee
you’ll get more out of it this way.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:12.790
The approach will be to cut the sphere into
many rings parallel to the xy plane, and to

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:19.029
compare the area of these rings to the area
of their shadows on the xy plane. All the

00:12:19.029 --> 00:12:23.920
shadows of the rings from the northern hemisphere
make up a circle with the same radius as the

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:29.870
sphere, right? The main idea will be to show
a correspondence between these ring shadows,

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:35.259
and every other ring on the sphere. Challenge
mode here is to pause now and see if you can

00:12:35.259 --> 00:12:38.650
predict how that might go.

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:44.769
We’ll label each one of these rings based
on the angle theta between a line from the

00:12:44.769 --> 00:12:54.570
sphere’s center to the ring and the z-axis.
So theta ranges from 0 to 180 degrees, which

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:59.880
is to say from 0 to pi radians. And let’s
call the change in angle from one ring to

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:05.250
the next d-theta, which means the thickness
of one of these rings with be the radius,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:06.300
R, times d-theta.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:12.690
Alright, structured exercise time. We’ll
ease in with a warm-up

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:19.230
Question #1: What is the circumference of
this ring at the inner edge, in terms of R

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:28.170
and theta? Go ahead and multiply your answer
the thickness R*d-theta to get an approximation

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:32.930
for this ring’s area; and approximation
that gets better and better as you chop up

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:36.500
the sphere more and more finely.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:42.759
At this point, if you know your calculus,
you could integrate. But our goal is not just

00:13:42.759 --> 00:13:49.000
to find the answer, it’s to feel the connection
between the sphere its shadow. So…

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:55.690
Question #2: What is the area of the shadow
of one of these rings on the xy-plane? Again,

00:13:55.690 --> 00:14:09.779
expressed in terms of R, theta and d-theta.

00:14:09.779 --> 00:14:16.840
Question #3: Each of these ring shadows has
precisely half the area of one of these rings

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:23.170
on the sphere. It’s not the one at angle
theta straight above it, but another one.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:26.070
Which one?
(As a hint, you might want to reference some

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:37.160
trig identities)
Question #4: I said in the outset there is

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:41.800
a correspondence between all the shadows from
the northern hemisphere, which make up a circle

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:47.889
with radius R, and every other ring on the
sphere. Use your answer to the last question

00:14:47.889 --> 00:14:57.060
to spell out exactly what that correspondence
is.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.899
Question #5: Bring it on home, why does this
imply that the area of the circle is exactly

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:08.350
¼ the surface area of the sphere, particularly
as we consider thinner and thinner rings?

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:12.649
If you want answers or hints, I’m quite
sure people in the comments and on reddit

00:15:12.649 --> 00:15:15.230
will have them waiting for you.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:19.470
And finally, I’d be remiss not to make a
brief mention of the fact that the surface

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:25.149
area of a sphere is a specific instance of
a much more general fact: If you take any

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:31.009
convex shape, and look at the average area
of all its shadows, averaged over all possible

00:15:31.009 --> 00:15:37.080
orientations in 3d space, the surface area
of the solid will be precisely 4 times that

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.050
average shadow area.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:47.220
As to why this is true, I’ll leave those
details for another day.

